The 660 vector usually means `memory ECC error', I think. In newer versions
of NetBSD we decode some of this. It's hard, because DEC doesn't document
what the codes mean. (#$*#!@@!) Fortunately, we can piece it togther,
sometimes, using various header files and source code that have been made
redistributable.


